    "group": "101-200",
    "title": "Surrounded Regions",
    "workload": "build one 300x300 grid at ~50% 'O' density once, then 400 punches; each punch copies the pristine grid into a working grid and runs the full solve (border flood-fill with an explicit position stack, then the relabel sweep) and hashes the result. The copy is deliberately inside the measured region: solve mutates the board, and the alternatives are a per-iteration refill loop or extra un-mutation work; a straight copy is the same O(rows*cols) order as the solve and every lane pays it identically. Density is load-bearing -- much sparser and the floods terminate immediately, much denser and the whole grid becomes one border-connected component. NOTE: all four lanes use a genuinely nested board (array of row pointers / Vec<Vec> / [][]), not a flat block, so C gets no locality advantage.",
    "sink": "806839188"
